var e=1,t=`alts-multistrat-01`,n=`alternatives`,r=`Multi-Strategy Funds`,i=`Several strategies inside one vehicle, with capital moved between them — and a single balance sheet that ties them together.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`alts-lse-01`,`alts-macro-01`],s=10,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A [[multi-strategy|multi-strategy]] fund runs several distinct strategies within a single vehicle — long/short equity, [[merger-arbitrage]], credit, [[global-macro]], [[statistical-arbitrage]] — under one risk framework and one balance sheet.

Two structural models exist and the difference matters more than the label suggests.

**The [[pod]] or platform model.** Many semi-autonomous teams, each running a defined book with its own risk limits and its own economics. The centre allocates capital, sets limits, controls financing, and removes teams that breach or underperform. The centre's skill is capital allocation and risk control, not investing.

**The integrated model.** One investment team operating across strategies, with capital shifted according to a shared view of where opportunity lies. The centre's skill is judgement about relative opportunity.

The attraction of either is straightforward: diversification across return sources, plus the ability to move capital toward whichever strategy currently has the best opportunity set — something an investor allocating to single-strategy funds cannot do at the same speed.`},{type:`concept`,id:`return-driver`,body:`**There are two distinct return drivers, and they should be assessed separately.**

**Strategy-level returns.** Whatever each underlying book generates. This is simply the sum of the constituent strategies, and it is what most investors think they are buying.

**Capital allocation.** The value the centre adds — or destroys — by moving capital between strategies. Increasing merger arbitrage capital when spreads widen, cutting a strategy whose edge is decaying, adding risk when opportunity is abundant.

That second driver is the entire reason to pay a multi-strategy fee rather than assembling the strategies yourself. So the diligence question is unavoidably direct: **is there evidence the centre allocates capital well?**

It is a harder question than it appears. Most multi-strategy funds cannot cleanly separate the two effects, and a fund that has performed well because its equity book performed well has not demonstrated allocation skill at all. The honest test is whether the centre has ever cut a well-liked, well-performing team on the judgement that the opportunity had thinned — and whether it added to something uncomfortable at the right time.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`one-balance-sheet`,body:`Here is the structural fact that makes multi-strategy different from a portfolio of separate funds, and it cuts both ways.

**It is one balance sheet.** Losses in one strategy consume capital available to all the others.

On the upside, that is genuinely efficient. Capital not needed by one book supports another; financing is arranged centrally on better terms; the netting of exposures across strategies reduces total margin required. A dollar works harder inside one vehicle than split across five.

On the downside, it removes the firewall an investor would otherwise have. If you hold five separate funds and one blows up, you lose that allocation. If a multi-strategy fund's credit book blows up, the resulting margin calls and forced deleveraging can require selling positions in the equity book — **liquidating strategies that were performing perfectly well** because the balance sheet needed the capital.

So the diversification is real at the level of return sources and much weaker at the level of catastrophic risk. **You are diversified across strategies and concentrated in one financing structure and one risk framework.** That is a trade worth making, but it should be made knowingly.`},{type:`table`,id:`environments`,caption:`What helps and what hurts`,headers:[`Environment`,`Effect`,`Why`],rows:[[`Dispersed opportunity across strategies`,`Helps most`,`The flexibility to move capital is worth most when some strategies are rich and others cheap.`],[`Stable financing relationships`,`Essential`,`Central financing is a structural advantage until it becomes a single point of failure.`],[`Rising volatility with functioning markets`,`Helps`,`More opportunity in most constituent strategies at once, with the ability to press the best of them.`],[`Correlated stress across strategies`,`Hurts badly`,`The strategies were diversified on average and share one balance sheet in stress.`],[`A single large loss in one book`,`Hurts badly`,`Consumes capital and can force liquidation of unrelated, healthy positions.`],[`Talent departure at the pod level`,`Hurts`,`In the platform model the returns belong substantially to individual teams, who can and do leave.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-blows-up`,body:`**Correlated stress across strategies.** The strategies were selected to be diversified, and diversification measured on average conditions thins out in stress — a pattern you have now met in almost every strategy in this domain. When several books lose simultaneously, the balance sheet takes the combined hit.

**Contagion through the balance sheet.** Losses in one book force deleveraging in others. The mechanism is margin, not investment judgement, and it means healthy positions get sold because unhealthy ones needed capital.

**Leverage at both levels.** Individual pods run leverage, and the fund may lever the aggregate. That compounds, and total effective leverage can be considerably higher than any single book's figure suggests.

**Opacity.** Multi-strategy funds are among the hardest to see into. Aggregated risk reports can conceal that three supposedly separate books hold correlated exposures. Asking for exposures *by strategy* rather than in aggregate is a reasonable and revealing request.

**Key person and pod risk.** In the platform model, a departing team takes its returns with it. High pod turnover is worth understanding: is it disciplined risk management removing underperformers, or an environment people leave?

**Fee layering.** Some structures charge at both pod and fund level, or pass through substantial expenses. Two teams can net off against each other's performance while both are paid, which is a genuine drag that headline fee terms may not reveal.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`smooth-returns`,body:`**Smooth multi-strategy returns can reflect diversification or offsetting mistakes.** If one book gains 4% while another loses 4%, the fund reports zero — indistinguishable from a fund that took no risk, and quite different in what it says about the manager.

So aggregate return and volatility are unusually uninformative for this structure. The useful information is the **attribution by strategy over time**: which books contributed, how consistently, and whether the centre's capital allocation moved toward the ones that worked. A fund unable or unwilling to provide that has removed your ability to assess the thing you are paying for.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask for attribution by strategy, over several years.** This is the single most valuable document in multi-strategy diligence. It shows whether returns come from one book or many, and whether allocation changes anticipated or followed performance.

**Ask what the centre has cut, and when.** A centre that has never reduced a strategy has never demonstrated the skill you are paying for. The best answer describes cutting something that was performing, on the judgement that the opportunity had thinned.

**Ask how a large loss in one pod would affect the others.** The answer describes the contagion mechanism. If the manager has not thought about it in those terms, you have learned something important.

**Understand the fee structure completely, including pass-through expenses.** Layered fees and expense pass-throughs can make headline terms misleading by a wide margin, and the netting problem — paying winning pods while losing pods offset them — is real.

**Ask about pod turnover and why people left.** In a platform model this is close to a question about the durability of the returns themselves.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Two return drivers: the underlying strategies, and the centre's capital allocation. Only the second justifies the fee, and only the second is hard to verify.`,`One balance sheet means efficient capital use and no firewall — losses in one book can force liquidation of healthy positions in another.`,`Diversified across return sources, concentrated in one financing structure and one risk framework.`,`Aggregate returns are uninformative here. Attribution by strategy over time is the document that matters.`]}],u=[{id:`alts-multistrat-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`multi-strategy`,`return-driver`,`fees`],concept:`return-driver`,stem:`What justifies paying a multi-strategy fee rather than allocating to single-strategy funds yourself?`,choices:[`Multi-strategy funds have lower fees than single-strategy funds`,`The centre's ability to move capital between strategies as opportunity shifts`,`Multi-strategy funds are less volatile by definition`,`Multi-strategy funds have better access to individual securities`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Multi-strategy fees are typically equal or higher, sometimes with layering and expense pass-throughs on top.`,`Correct. The underlying strategy returns you could in principle assemble yourself. What you cannot replicate is reallocating capital between them at speed, which is the centre's claimed skill.`,`Lower volatility is a hoped-for consequence of diversification, not a definitional property — and it can equally reflect offsetting mistakes.`,`Security access is not the advantage. The constituent strategies would have similar access run standalone.`],explanation:`Being clear about what you are paying for turns diligence into a specific question: show me evidence the centre allocates capital well. If the answer is only "our returns were good", the fee has not been justified.`},{id:`alts-multistrat-01-q2`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:3,tags:[`multi-strategy`,`diversification`,`misconception`],concept:`one-balance-sheet`,stem:`"A multi-strategy fund running five diversified strategies is safer than allocating to five separate single-strategy funds." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — five separate funds give you a firewall; one balance sheet means a loss in any book can force liquidation of the others`,`False — multi-strategy funds are always riskier than single-strategy funds`,`True — internal diversification with central risk management is strictly better`,`False — because five strategies is not enough diversification`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Holding separate funds means a blow-up costs you that allocation and no more. Inside one vehicle, margin calls from one book can force selling in unrelated, healthy books.`,`Too absolute. The single vehicle also brings real advantages — netting, central financing, faster reallocation. It is a trade, not a ranking.`,`This is the misconception. Central risk management is genuinely valuable, and it does not create the firewall that separate legal entities do.`,`Five strategies can be reasonable diversification. The issue is the shared balance sheet, not the count.`],explanation:`The distinction is between diversification of *return sources* and diversification of *catastrophic risk*. Multi-strategy delivers the first well and the second poorly, which is a defensible trade to make knowingly and a nasty surprise to discover afterwards.`},{id:`alts-multistrat-01-q3`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`multi-strategy`,`attribution`,`on-the-job`],concept:`smooth-returns`,stem:`A multi-strategy fund reports 0.2% for the quarter. Why is that figure nearly uninformative?`,choices:[`Quarterly returns are always too short a period to interpret`,`It could reflect several books contributing modestly, or large gains in one offset by large losses in another`,`Multi-strategy funds do not report accurate quarterly figures`,`The figure is net of fees, so the gross number is what matters`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Length is a general caveat, but the specific problem here is aggregation across books rather than the reporting period.`,`Correct. A flat aggregate is consistent with a fund that took almost no risk and with one whose books moved violently in opposite directions. Those are very different situations and the headline cannot distinguish them.`,`There is no reason to doubt the accuracy. The problem is what the number omits, not whether it is right.`,`Gross versus net matters for fee analysis but does not address the aggregation problem at all.`],explanation:`Aggregation hides more in this structure than in any other. Attribution by strategy is what converts an uninterpretable number into information about whether the manager is doing what they are paid for.`},{id:`alts-multistrat-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`multi-strategy`,`contagion`,`how-it-blows-up`],concept:`how-it-blows-up`,stem:`A multi-strategy fund's credit book suffers a severe loss. Why might its well-performing equity book be liquidated as a result?`,choices:[`Because the equity book must have had correlated positions`,`Because the loss consumes capital and triggers margin calls, and the equity positions are the most liquid source of cash`,`Because investors will redeem specifically from the equity strategy`,`Because the risk system will automatically close all positions`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The books may have been genuinely uncorrelated. The liquidation is driven by the financing structure, not by shared exposure.`,`Correct. One balance sheet means the credit loss reduces capital supporting everything. Meeting margin calls means selling what can be sold quickly — which is usually the healthy, liquid equity book, precisely because it is healthy and liquid.`,`Investors redeem from the fund, not from a strategy within it. They have no mechanism to target one book.`,`Risk systems reduce exposure selectively rather than closing everything, and it is the margin requirement driving the choice.`],explanation:`There is a bitter logic to it: the healthiest, most liquid book is the first sold, because it is the easiest to sell. Good positions are liquidated because they are good positions in a liquid market, and that is the contagion mechanism in one sentence.`},{id:`alts-multistrat-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`multi-strategy`,`fees`,`netting`],concept:`how-it-blows-up`,stem:`In a platform fund, one pod gains 20% and another loses 20% on equal capital. The fund is flat, yet performance fees are paid. Why?`,choices:[`The fund is misreporting its returns`,`Some structures pay performance fees at the pod level, so the winning pod is paid while the losing pod's shortfall is borne by the investor`,`Performance fees are always charged regardless of returns`,`The losing pod must repay its previous fees`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Nothing is misreported. The outcome follows directly from where the fee is calculated.`,`Correct. This is the netting problem. When fees are struck pod by pod rather than on the fund's aggregate result, an investor can pay meaningful performance fees in a year the fund made nothing.`,`Performance fees normally require performance — the point here is *whose* performance the calculation uses.`,`Clawbacks of previously paid fees are rare and would not typically apply to a pod's current-year loss.`],explanation:`Where the fee is calculated is as important as its headline rate. Whether performance fees net across pods is a specific, answerable question, and the answer can be worth several percent a year.`},{id:`alts-multistrat-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`multi-strategy`,`allocation-skill`,`diligence`],concept:`return-driver`,stem:`What would be the strongest evidence that a multi-strategy centre genuinely adds value through capital allocation?`,choices:[`Several consecutive years of strong aggregate returns`,`A record of cutting capital from a well-performing strategy on the judgement that its opportunity had thinned`,`A large number of strategies running simultaneously`,`Low aggregate volatility relative to peers`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Strong aggregate returns are consistent with one book doing well and the centre doing nothing. It does not isolate allocation skill.`,`Correct. Cutting something that is working is the hardest allocation decision to make and the least likely to happen by accident. It is close to the only observable evidence that separates allocation judgement from luck.`,`Strategy count measures breadth, not judgement. More books can equally mean less oversight of each.`,`Low volatility may reflect diversification, offsetting mistakes, or simply less risk taken. It says nothing about allocation skill.`],explanation:`Adding to a winner is easy and often momentum-following. Cutting a winner because the opportunity has narrowed requires a forward-looking judgement that can be wrong and visible — which is exactly why it is the credible evidence.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:10,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
